# Project instructions

- Read `.gig/JOB.md` before working. Price and payment facts come only from `.gig/QUOTE.md`; quotes in old reports are void.
- Original materials are read-only{% if material_path %}: `{{ material_path }}`{% endif %}. Copy needed samples to `data/`; client data is never committed.
- New results go to new directories, never overwriting old ones; failures and unusable states stay visible.
- Never tune numbers toward the client's expectations or a paper. Reported figures come from saved results, with the data split, configuration, seeds and aggregation stated.
- At the end of every phase append to "Status" in `.gig/JOB.md`: commands, verification figures, commit, remaining work, whether anything was sent out.
- Temporary files and recovery copies go in `.scratch/`, cleared at the end of a phase. Create nothing in the parent directory.
- Actions that need JC's explicit approval: sending anything out, pushing to a remote, deleting or archiving, paid remote resources, scope changes beyond the confirmed decisions, edits to `.gig/QUOTE.md`.
- ASCII punctuation in prose. English directory and file names. Explain concepts with concrete names, no undefined abbreviations.
- Reports use Kami or LaTeX; keep only the final PDF; sources in `.scratch/reports/`, prose through sepia.
- Commit each verified unit of work locally; before a long experiment commit the code, configuration and evaluation protocol, and bind the run manifest to that commit. Pushing needs separate authorisation.

## CV / ML projects

- Training and heavy computation run on the remote GPU server, with environments and caches on the data disk (see the shanhe-computing-server skill). Run training only when the current request explicitly includes experiments; editing a report does not authorise training.
- Keep raw runs, checkpoints, predictions and experiment summaries; derive reported numbers from saved results. Keep only thin copies locally.
- Write the evaluation set and acceptance criteria (metrics, thresholds, sample counts) into JOB.md "Confirmed decisions" before experimenting. Results go under `results/evaluation/`, never under `.gig/`.
- Subjective or image-based acceptance keeps manual samples, failure examples and known measurement gaps.
- Configuration through Hydra and YAML, every parameter listed, nothing silently falling back to defaults. Every configuration item can be overridden on the command line.

